\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#stable}{\figure{lifecycle-stable.svg}{options: alt='[Stable]'}}}{\strong{[Stable]}}
Carries out the sequence of tests Steyn (2009) proposed for the Solomon
four-group design and for its extension to several interventions, the
extended Solomon design: k interventions and a control, each with and
without a pretest, giving 2(k + 1) groups. The sequence checks the
threats to internal validity the design can detect (nonequivalent groups,
history and maturation, testing, the pretest-intervention interaction,
instrumentation, regression to the mean, and attrition) before it
compares the interventions. It is a published
proposal, kept for replication and teaching. The package's recommended
analysis is \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} with \verb{control =}, which estimates the
Solomon contrasts of every intervention in one model.
}
\section{Steyn's sequence}{

Steyn labels the scores by group: \code{Oa1}, \code{Ob1} are the pretest and
posttest of the pretested group of intervention 1 (EG1); \code{Oc}, \code{Od} the
pretest and posttest of the pretested control (CG1); \code{Oe1} the posttest
of the unpretested group of intervention 1 (CG2.1); and \code{Of} the posttest
of the unpretested control (CG3). With one intervention the number is
dropped (\code{Oa}, \code{Ob}, \code{Oe}; EG, CG2). The steps below follow a
pre-publication draft of the article, dated March 2, 2009 (see Source).
In Steyn's order:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Equivalence after randomization.} The pretests of the pretested
groups are compared: a t test for one intervention, a one-way ANOVA
otherwise. If they differ, Steyn advises post hoc tests to locate the
difference, an explanation, and reconsidering whether to continue.
With \code{include_unpretested_control = TRUE}, a further one-way ANOVA adds
\code{Of}, which Steyn suggests when the study is short or history and
maturation are believed negligible.
\item \strong{History and maturation.} A paired t test of \code{Oc} and \code{Od}; a t test
of all the pretests (the Time 1 value for the unpretested control)
against \code{Of}; and a one-way ANOVA of \code{Oc}, \code{Od}, and \code{Of}. If \code{Oc}
differs from \code{Od} and \code{Of}, Steyn reads history or maturation; if \code{Od}
differs from \code{Oc} and \code{Of}, the pretest (a testing effect). Steyn
(2009) presents this reading of the three sets of scores (\code{Oc}, \code{Od},
and \code{Of}) as new.
\item \strong{Testing effect.} The pretest main effect in the two-way
between-groups ANOVA of the four posttest groups of each intervention
and the control.
\item \strong{Pretest-intervention interaction.} The decision sequence of Walton
Braver and Braver (1988), \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}, for each
intervention against the control.
\item \strong{Test-retest reliability and instrumentation.} The correlation of
\code{Oc} and \code{Od}; the paired t test of \code{Oc} and \code{Od}; and a t test of \code{Oc}
and \code{Of}.
\item \strong{Regression to the mean.} A chi-square test for the variance of
\code{Od} against that of \code{Oc}. Steyn reads an increase in variance as a
sign of regression to the mean, a threat when groups were selected
for extreme scores.
\item \strong{Attrition.} A z test of the dropout proportions of the
intervention groups against the non-intervention groups (CG1 and CG3),
and Steyn's chi-square on the two-by-two table of dropouts,
intervention or not by pretested or not (df = 1). That chi-square asks
whether intervention and pretesting are associated among the dropouts:
whether the dropouts of the intervention groups were pretested more,
or less, often than the dropouts of the non-intervention groups. It
counts dropouts only, so it does not compare dropout rates, and it
depends on how many participants each group started with. To compare
dropout rates by group, see \code{attrition$counts} and
\code{\link[=check_solomon_missing]{check_solomon_missing()}}, which counts the missing posttests in each
group.
\item \strong{Effects of the interventions.} For one intervention: E1, a one-way
ANOVA of the four posttest groups, and E2, a t test of \code{Ob} plus \code{Oe}
against \code{Od} plus \code{Of}; Steyn (2009) credits these two comparisons to
his doctoral thesis (Steyn, 2001, as cited in Steyn, 2009). For
several:
\itemize{
\item E1, a one-way ANOVA of all 2(k + 1) posttest groups;
\item E2, post hoc tests of every pair of groups, summarized by whether
each intervention group differs from \code{Od} and from \code{Of};
\item E3, a one-way ANOVA of the 2k intervention groups;
\item E4, for each intervention, a t test of \code{Ob} against \code{Oe};
\item E5, when no E4 test is significant, a one-way ANOVA of the k
interventions with their two groups combined, with post hoc tests,
which Steyn uses to find which intervention had the greatest
effect. When an E4 test is significant, Steyn cautions that
internal validity is in question and the groups are not combined.
}
}

Every test is computed when the data allow it; \code{path} records the steps
Steyn's sequence acts on at \code{alpha}. Steps 1, 2, 4, 5, and 6 use the
pretest and are skipped, with a note, when \code{y_pre} is \code{NULL}.
}

\section{Operationalization}{

Steyn's description leaves these choices open. solomonR made them as
follows.
\itemize{
\item \strong{t tests.} Independent-samples t tests are Student's pooled-variance
tests, matching the equal-variance one-way ANOVAs.
\item \strong{Post hoc tests.} Steyn (2009) names no post hoc test. In the study
the article describes, Steyn (2005, Table 5.61, p. 153) used Scheffé
tests after the one-way ANOVA of the eight posttest groups, so they are
the default (\code{posthoc = "scheffe"}). They are the method of Scheffé
(1953). For a pair of groups among G groups with N scores in all, the
Scheffé statistic is the squared pairwise t divided by G - 1, on G - 1
and N - G degrees of freedom, with the pooled error variance of the
groups in the ANOVA. The pair differs at \code{alpha} exactly when
Scheffé's criterion holds: |t| exceeds S, where S^2 is G - 1 times the
upper \code{alpha} point of F(G - 1, N - G) (Scheffé, 1953, pp. 87–89, 97).
Scheffé's procedure protects every contrast among the groups, not only
the pairs, so it is conservative when only pairs are compared; for
pairs of equally precise means (equal group sizes), Scheffé
recommended Tukey's method instead (pp. 89, 92–93, 96–97). Because the
F test rejects exactly when some contrast is significant (pp. 87,
95–96), no pair can differ unless the ANOVA of the same groups is
significant, and a significant ANOVA need not yield a pair that
differs; step 2 then reports a pattern Steyn's rule does not cover.
\code{posthoc = "holm"} gives pairwise t tests with the same pooled SD and
Holm's (1979) adjustment, the default of \code{\link[stats:pairwise.t.test]{stats::pairwise.t.test()}},
which covers the pairwise comparisons only. In step 1 the post hoc
tests follow a significant ANOVA of more than two groups; in step 2,
and in step 8 with several interventions (E2 and E5), they are always
computed.
\item \strong{Two-way ANOVA.} Type III sums of squares, with effect coding, one
analysis for each intervention against the control.
\item \strong{History classification.} Two sets "differ" when their post hoc
p-value (Scheffé's, or Holm-adjusted) is below \code{alpha}. A
nonsignificant ANOVA gives "no evidence of history, maturation, or a
testing effect"; \code{Oc} differing from \code{Od} and from \code{Of}, which do not
differ, gives "history or maturation"; \code{Od} differing from \code{Oc} and
from \code{Of}, which do not differ, gives "the pretest (a testing effect)";
any other pattern is reported as one Steyn's rule does not cover.
\item \strong{Scores used.} The one-way ANOVA of \code{Oc}, \code{Od}, and \code{Of} and the t
test of \code{Oc} and \code{Of} use every available score in each set. The paired
t test, the correlation, and the variance test use the pretested
controls with both scores.
\item \strong{Variance test.} The statistic is (n - 1) times the variance of \code{Od}
divided by the variance of \code{Oc}, on n - 1 degrees of freedom, with a
two-sided p-value (twice the smaller tail).
\item \strong{Attrition.} A participant with a missing posttest is a dropout.
"Intervention" means any intervention. The z test pools the two
proportions; neither test has a continuity correction. The
chi-square is computed on the dropout counts, as Steyn describes; a
note is recorded when an expected count is below 5.
\item \strong{"Differs from both control groups"} (E2). An intervention group
differs from both when its post hoc p-values against \code{Od} and against
\code{Of} are both below \code{alpha}. The sequence continues to E3 when at least
one intervention group does.
\item \strong{"Greatest effect"} (E5). Steyn does not say how the intervention
with the greatest effect is identified. solomonR reports the
intervention with the highest combined posttest mean (\code{highest}) and
the post hoc tests of every pair. The highest mean is the greatest
effect only when the interventions raise the scores; when they lower
them, as in Steyn (2005), the greatest effect is the lowest mean.
Read \code{highest} with the direction of the outcome and the post hoc
tests. In the English report of that study, Steyn and Mynhardt (2008,
pp. 569–570) ranked the treatments by the difference between treated
and untreated means and by d, from separate 2 x 2 analyses, without a
test of the differences between treatments.
\item \strong{Missing scores.} Participants with a missing posttest stay in the
data for the attrition step and are left out of the posttest analyses.
The pretest analyses use the pretested participants with a pretest.
\item \strong{Tests that cannot be computed.} A test of groups that are empty,
too small, or without variation is left out, with a note in \code{notes}.
\item \strong{Step 4} uses \code{fit_solomon_classic(flow = "1988")} with its defaults
(ANCOVA as the pretested-groups test, and Test I).
}
}

\section{Cautions}{

\itemize{
\item Steyn notes that multiple ANOVAs capitalize on chance. The sequence
runs many tests, each at \code{alpha}, with no control of the error rate
across them.
\item A nonsignificant test is not evidence that groups are equivalent, or
that a threat is absent.
\item The one-way ANOVA of \code{Oc}, \code{Od}, and \code{Of}, and its post hoc tests,
treat the paired scores of \code{Oc} and \code{Od}, which come from the same
participants, as independent. Scheffé's (1953, p. 87) tests take the
covariances of the group means as known; here that of \code{Oc} and \code{Od} is
taken to be zero.
\item The chi-square test for the variance treats the variance of \code{Oc} as a
known value and ignores the pairing of \code{Oc} and \code{Od}.
\item A difference between \code{Ob} and \code{Oe} (E4) mixes a pretest main effect
with pretest sensitization. Step 4 tests the interaction for each
intervention separately; the joint model (\code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}) tests it
for all the interventions in one model, with adjusted p-values for the
comparisons.
\item The package's recommended analysis is \code{fit_solomon_glm(control = )}.
}
}

\section{Source}{

This function follows a pre-publication draft of Steyn's (2009)
article, dated March 2, 2009, which the author provided (R. Steyn,
personal communication, September 30, 2026). The published article was
not available for comparison. The draft has no page numbers, so none are
cited.
}

